<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jebel Akhdar War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jebel Akhdar rebellion · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jebel Akhdar War was a conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> between the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and Oman and the Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Oman in the interior, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Ghalib ibn Ali. Disputes over oil exploration led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur">Said bin Taimur</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> in 1955. The rebels held out on the Jebel Akhdar plateau until British special forces stormed it in 1959, ending the imamate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jebel-akhdar-war.jpg" alt="Jebel Akhdar War" width="320" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Altatoron; derivative work by Kolomaznik · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maskat_%26_Oman_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> had long chosen its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> by election, while from the 19th century a hereditary line of Al Busaidi sultans ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>. Britain backed the Muscat monarchy from the late 18th century and built up its influence through treaties, loans and British officials who held most of the sultan's ministries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-seeb">Treaty of Seeb</a> recognised the autonomy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a>.</p><p>From 1945 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur">Said bin Taimur</a> planned, with British support, to take over the interior after the death of Imam Alkhalili, so that the Iraq Petroleum Company could explore for oil there; the company financed the force he raised. Alkhalili died on 3 May 1954, and Ghalib al-Hinai, a judge who had served as his assistant, was elected imam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 10 October 1954 the sultan licensed the company's prospectors to search near Fahud, inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a>'s territory, and sent forces to occupy it, which the Imamate held to be a breach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-seeb">Treaty of Seeb</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and Oman Field Force, which had eight British officers, took Adam in December 1954, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> was captured on 15 December 1955. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>'s brother Talib al-Hinai fled to Saudi Arabia and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and the Imamate found backing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Saudi Arabia.</p><p>In 1957 Talib returned with 300 fighters who landed on the Albatinah coast. Joined by Imam Ghalib, his forces took Bahla Fort, and Suleiman bin Himyar, sheikh of one of the main tribes of the interior, rose against the sultan. The Field Force was ambushed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanuf">Tanuf</a>, Kamah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> and largely destroyed in its retreat, leaving only Ibri under the sultan's control. In August 1957 the UN Security Council voted 5 to 4 not to hold an urgent meeting on the Arab states' charge of British aggression.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>British papers declassified later showed that air strikes deliberately destroyed aflaj irrigation channels and crops, and that the Foreign Secretary approved strikes without prior warning on 4 August 1957. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> flew 2,080 sorties and dropped 1,750 tons of bombs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s Middle East Development Division estimated that 90 percent of the houses on the Jebel Akhdar were damaged. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>'s defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-seeb">Treaty of Seeb</a> was ended and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> abolished, though it kept a government in exile and an office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p>The UN General Assembly adopted resolutions on the Question of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> in 1965, 1966 and 1967, calling on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to end its control, and the question stayed on its agenda until 1971. Insurgents crossing from Saudi Arabia went on laying land mines, and the Oman Gendarmerie was formed in 1960 to help stop them. Britain kept the air bases at Salalah and Masirah until 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jebel Akhdar War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan Said bin Taimur's forces, backed by Britain, occupied Nizwa in 1955, and the <b>Jebel Akhdar War</b> ended in 1959 when British troops stormed the mountain.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-oman">Imamate of Oman</a></li><li><blockquote>Said bin Taimur, sultan from 1932, kept the country isolated, won the <b>Jebel Akhdar War</b> of 1954-1959 and sold Gwadar to Pakistan in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a></li></ul></section>
